  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/monostichodus-mesmaekersi-2/",
      "html": "<p>The genus Monostichodus (formerly Hemistichodus) belongs to the subfamily Ichthyborinae (fin eaters). The maximum length of M. mesmaekersi is about 6 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/monostichodus-mesmaekersi-2/",
      "html": "<p>This tetra comes from the Congo.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/monostichodus-mesmaekersi-2/",
      "html": "<p>Many members of the subfamily are specialised fish that feed almost exclusively on parts of fins bitten off other fish. At least M. mesmaekersi seems not to do this: it was kept with fantail goldfish, bettas, killifish and knifefish, and the fins of all tankmates, as well as those of conspecifics, stayed unharmed. The only report known about keeping the fish says they proved to be scale-eaters. This can be neither confirmed nor denied, but the fish listed above were not attacked for their scales either.</p><p>The fish are not shy at all and their behaviour is much like that of pencilfishes (Nannostomus) from South America.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/monostichodus-mesmaekersi-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species is practically unknown among aquarists. Every individual seems to have a slightly different pattern on the caudal fin. This beautiful tetra is a challenge for every enthusiast who likes to be an explorer.</p>"
    }
  ],
